{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261124T044504Z-ac4ab539", "ts": "2026-11-24T04:52:42Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "feature/boundary-coverage-check", "build": "09a262e0ed69"}, "body": {"base_commit": "09a262e0ed69fc486c15573632f694793d97b296", "base_tree": "5fbcbe8143840faa6af0896aa0f652a8bdf34fe5", "head_tree": "87028f59fc2a549820ec248a38d960d921f0ad41", "head_commit": "78ddc3cf74aeb34490baf0ed61a20bea27e5ad1d", "dispatch_commit": "78ddc3cf74aeb34490baf0ed61a20bea27e5ad1d", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": [".testament.toml", "documentation/release-process.md", "documentation/running-tests.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "pyproject.toml", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-boundary-coverage-check.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-check.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", ".testament.toml", "documentation/release-process.md", "documentation/running-tests.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "pyproject.toml", "tests/test_boundary_coverage.py", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-boundary-coverage-check.md", ".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-check.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The advice can still move check-releasability's exit: an exception inside classify_pending escapes into the gate", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `_boundary_coverage_lines` calls `boundary_coverage.classify_pending` with no containment, so anything the advice raises unwinds `check_releasability` before the suite verdict, the version and the classification run. The hook's `main()` floor then turns it into `error: check-releasability: ...` and exit 1. The comment at the call site says the advice is 'never part of the verdict, so it cannot move the exit', and the plan's architecture disposition says 'advice fails soft \u2192 conforms ... the check-releasability line never changes its exit'. One concrete path exists today. `read_facts` and `append_fact` (and the envelope import behind `evidence sync`) check only that `body` is a dict, never the type of `base_tree`/`head_tree`. `coverage_algebra` defends against that on purpose (`isinstance(head, str) and head in nodes`). `_Classifier._boundary_split` (`split not in branch_trees`) and `_batch_review` (`trees_at.get(body.get(\"base_tree\"))`) do not, so a single review fact whose tree field is a list or dict raises TypeError (unhashable). The shared append-only store keeps that fact, so the release gate keeps failing until someone edits the store by hand. Hook-surface rule, 'A docstring or comment is a claim: before one states reach or a guarantee ... grep the callers or test the path'. Fix the class through its one owner: contain the advice at `_boundary_coverage_lines`, catching any exception and printing the existing `NOTE: boundary-coverage: could not evaluate \u2014 ...` line. Add a test that makes `classify_pending` raise and asserts the verdict and exit are unchanged. Separately, guard the two tree lookups with the algebra's `isinstance(..., str)` test so the op itself reads such a fact as absent rather than crashing.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "Any exception raised inside boundary_coverage.classify_pending, or anything it calls, that reaches check_releasability uncaught.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py::_boundary_coverage_lines (uncontained call)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py::_Classifier._boundary_split (split not in branch_trees)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py::_Classifier._batch_review (trees_at.get on an unvalidated tree value)"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The release unit misses judgeable first-parent commits that add no fragment, so 'N of N covered' can sit over unreviewed work", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The plan's Goals mark this as an inference for the boundary review to check ('the release unit is the pending fragment set'). Checked, it holds for PR-flow merges only. `check-change-log-entry` refuses `fragment-edited-not-added`, so a branch merged through `/prawduct:pr` with judgeable work always adds a fragment and gets classified. Two other kinds of first-parent commit on the base change judgeable files and add no fragment: a direct commit, and a PR merged outside the skill (the gate lives in the skill, not on the server). `classify_pending` iterates only `pending_scopes`, so neither kind enters the denominator. The count line, the release-process step ('Read a `direct` or `uncovered` scope as work no boundary review read before it ships') and the PR skill's 'Check that instead of assuming it' then read complete over work nothing reviewed. That is the unsafe direction the Goals forbid ('never in the other (`covered`)'), and wave 2 makes this same answer a gate for `per_release`. Measured on this repo since the v3.9.0 cut (57a1b47d): the two fragment-less first-parent commits (4a381e2d, 4b37f25f) change only non-judgeable files, so today's counts are right. Either bound the release interval by the newest first-parent commit that deleted fragments (the cut, observable in git), and classify every judgeable fragment-less first-parent commit after it as `direct`, or record the limit under the plan's Tradeoffs accepted, so wave 2 (#1232) inherits it as a stated gap instead of rediscovering it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A first-parent commit on the integration base, since the last fragment cut, that changes a judgeable file and adds no `.prawduct/changes/` fragment.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py::classify_pending (denominator is pending_scopes only)"}], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The planned test 'the release guard's prose names an op the dispatcher has' was not written", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Chunk 02's Tests list it, and nothing in the bundle implements it. `test_every_instructed_command_is_granted` pins that the PR skill's prose and its `allowed-tools` grant agree with each other, not that either names a real op. Rename or remove `check-boundary-coverage` in the dispatcher and the skill keeps telling the agent at a release branch to run a command that no longer exists, with the suite green. Add the test the plan names: read `plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md`'s release-promotion guard and assert the op it names is in the dispatch chain (`test_hook_argument_shape._dispatch_branches()` already derives that set).", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "tests/test_hook_argument_shape.py"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Advisory boundary-coverage classification runs inside check-releasability with no fault boundary", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the advice was put inside an authority gate's control flow, so anything the advice raises becomes the gate's outcome. `_boundary_coverage_lines` calls `boundary_coverage.classify_pending` from `check_releasability` without a guard, and `classify_pending` and `_Classifier` catch nothing. An exception raised from `coverage_algebra.coverage_verdict`, from the tree-key memo, or from a malformed fact body (for example `(fact.get('body') or {}).get(...)` in `_is_boundary` or `_is_verify` when the body is a non-dict) would turn a Phase 0 release gate into a traceback. The comment at the call site says the advice is 'never part of the verdict, so it cannot move the exit', and the plan says the line 'never changes its exit'. Neither holds on that path. The same escape makes the CLI op exit 1 with a traceback, where api-contract says 3 means 'could not evaluate'. The repo already has this boundary elsewhere: `gates.py` ~L2800 (`advice must never crash the gate that renders it`) and `coverage.py` ~L1576 (`advisory surfaces degrade on a failed composition`). Fix it with one guard at the owner: wrap the classification in `classify_pending`, behind a `prawduct:allow prawduct/broad-except` waiver whose reason is genuine, and return `Report('unevaluable', reason=f'{type(exc).__name__}: \u2026')`. Both consumers then degrade the same way, the op to exit 3 and check-releasability to its existing `NOTE: boundary-coverage: could not evaluate` line. Add a test that monkeypatches `coverage_algebra.coverage_verdict` to raise and asserts check-releasability's exit is unchanged.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The single call path from an exit-bearing surface into boundary_coverage.classify_pending without an exception boundary.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py:classify_pending"}], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The not-covered classification set is restated in prose, and release-process.md's copy omits inner-only and unknown", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: the op's human output does not say which lines are not covered, so every prose consumer has to restate the complement of `boundary_coverage.COVERED`, and the restatements already disagree. `plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md` tells the agent to relay scopes named `inner-only`, `uncovered`, `direct` or `unknown`. `documentation/release-process.md` tells the operator to 'Read a `direct` or `uncovered` scope as work no boundary review read before it ships'. That leaves out `inner-only`, which by its own definition is work only inner-stage reviews read, and it is the case #1223 wave 2 exists for. An operator following release-process.md reads an `inner-only` scope as fine and ships it unexamined. check-releasability alone derives the set from `COVERED` (its `NOTE:` lines), so it is correct. To find other members, search for prose naming boundary-coverage classifications as covered or not covered. A longer list does not close the class. A construction does: have `render` mark each not-covered entry from `COVERED`, for example with the same `NOTE: boundary-coverage:` prefix or a covered/not-covered column. Then make the skill and the doc relational ('relay each line the op marks not covered'; 'read each scope it marks not covered as work no boundary review read'), and pin the marker with a test.", "files": ["documentation/release-process.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "Any prose surface that enumerates which check-boundary-coverage classifications count as covered or not covered, instead of reading a marker the op derives from boundary_coverage.COVERED.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "documentation/release-process.md step 0 ('Read a `direct` or `uncovered` scope\u2026')"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md release-promotion guard ('(`inner-only`, `uncovered`, `direct`, `unknown`)')"}], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The boundary-coverage definition wave 2 will gate on rests on three unruled inferences, and the accepted design's section 3 still states the rule it superseded", "recommendation": "Instance. The plan's Goals list three items as 'Inferred (owner did not rule these; the boundary review should check them)': the release unit is the pending fragment set rather than 'each first-parent merge M since the last tag'; boundary coverage is a boundary-stage prefix followed by a verify-only tail, rather than design section 3's 'coverage_verdict restricted to boundary facts'; and the first-parent bracket rule for batch-covered. Read against the code, all three are what plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py implements (classify_commit requires the overall verdict to be covered as well as _boundary_split; _batch_review brackets on first-parent positions), and the stated reasons hold: 7 of 14 merges would read uncovered under section 3's rule. But they are still unruled, and decision 1 makes this classification the blocking release check under per_release. The second inference is the real fork, because it changes what 'a boundary review read this' means. The other two follow from the release mechanics and can be confirmed in bulk. The consequence: wave 2 has two specs that disagree. documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md section 3 is the owner-accepted spec ('Yes to all'), and it still says boundary-only composition since the last tag. The plan calls itself 'a correction to the design doc section 3', but the doc was never corrected. A wave-2 builder who works from the accepted spec rebuilds the rule this plan measured as wrong, or an owner ruling on wave 2 rests on a definition the owner never saw. The fix: get the owner's confirm-or-correct on the section-3 inference, with the other two in one bulk line, recorded where wave 2's builder will read it. Then make section 3 point at boundary_coverage's definition, or delete the superseded sentence there (authoring: 'A chunk boundary defers a DELETION, never a CORRECTION'; 'A ruling recorded only where the deciders read it is NOT recorded').", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The unruled Goals inferences of build-plan-boundary-coverage-check, and the design-doc sentence they supersede.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "documentation/issues/1222-1223-design.md section 3 (boundary-only composition, 'since the last tag')"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py module docstring and _boundary_split (the definition as implemented)"}], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "An 'uncovered: no review evidence composes' reading cannot tell an unreviewed merge from a clone that never ingested the review, and it names no remedy", "recommendation": "Instance. The evidence store is per clone (this plan's own Chunk 01 decision marks the real-store test requires_local_cache for that reason). Reviews recorded in another clone, by a teammate or on another machine, reach this one only through `prawduct-hook evidence sync`. When nothing composes, classify_commit returns UNCOVERED with 'no review evidence composes over the branch', and neither the human report nor --json says how many review facts the store holds. A release operator in a clone that is behind therefore reads every teammate's merge as work no boundary review read, and the PR skill's release guard, which ships to consumer repos, relays exactly that. The store's other missing-review readers already name the cause and the remedy: gates.py, around line 3087, says 'If that review was recorded in another clone, run `prawduct-hook evidence sync` to ingest it'. The wave-2 entry evidence, 'this op's first counts', is skewed the same way. The fix: when nothing composes, the reason should name `prawduct-hook evidence sync` as the way in for reviews recorded elsewhere, or the report should carry the store's review-fact count beside the count line, so an empty or behind store is visible as such. This keeps the safe-direction classification; only the reason changes.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The 'nothing composes' reason in _Classifier.classify_commit.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py _Classifier.classify_commit (final UNCOVERED return)"}], "fid": "R-7"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-artifact reconciliation: 'state-file growth ... inapplicable because the op writes no state' is not literally true", "recommendation": "Through `gates._tree_key_fn`, the op writes the shared tree-key memo (`tree_key_memo`, flushed at exit), and the plan's own Chunk 01 decision says so. The norm is still met, because the memo is capped (`MAX_ENTRIES`) and the write is the one `check-cumulative-critic` already makes. No departure, so no finding. The disposition's stated reason is just inaccurate. If the plan is touched again, 'conforms: the only write is the bounded tree-key memo the PR gate already makes' would be true. No edit is owed for this alone. The other 14 dispositions hold against the diff. The architecture disposition 'advice fails soft' is true by design, apart from the crash path in finding 1.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "`unknown`'s reason says 'not merged yet' when the merge is local but unpushed", "recommendation": "The base resolves to `origin/develop` (`coverage.repo_base_branch`, #311), while the pending set is read from the working tree. On a local `develop` carrying unpushed merges, those scopes read `unknown \u2014 no first-parent commit on origin/develop added its fragment (not merged yet, ...)`. That is the safe direction, and the reason names the base, so an operator can work it out. Wording only, and it changes no action.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The documented-invocation list in test_hook_argument_shape gained no entry for the new op", "recommendation": "`check-boundary-coverage` was added to the `listed` set that `test_every_dispatched_command_appears_in_the_documented_list` compares with the dispatch chain, but not to the `documented` invocations above it (for example `(\"check-boundary-coverage\", [\"--json\"])`). The pre-dispatch guard ignores argv-taking commands, and `_reject_unknown_args` in the wrapper is what refuses (pinned by `test_the_cli_refuses_an_unknown_argument`), so the entry would assert nothing new. No edit is wanted.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "read-cost: _batch_review rebuilds the first-parent index for every scope", "recommendation": "`_Classifier.first_parent()` is memoized. `_batch_review` still rebuilds `position` and `trees_at` over the whole first-parent history on every call, which is once per non-pr-covered scope, and it scans every boundary fact in the store each time. Its coverage verdicts also bypass the `verdict_cache.VerdictCache` that `coverage.py`'s advisory reader uses. This runs only at release time and no volume is stated, so it costs nobody anything today. Hoist the two indexes onto the classifier next to `_first_parent`. See `templates/data-model.md` 'Access Paths' if a volume is ever stated.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "check": "read-cost", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "release_readiness and boundary_coverage import each other, and the pending set is read twice", "recommendation": "`check_releasability` computes `pending_scopes` and then calls `classify_pending`. That lazily imports `release_readiness` back and recomputes `pending_scopes`, so the two modules form a cycle that the lazy imports hide. The comment 'No refusal line: this runs below check-releasability's refusal return' depends on two independent reads agreeing. Have `classify_pending` accept an optional already-computed `PendingSet` and pass it from check-releasability. That breaks the cycle at that call and makes the comment's invariant structural.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "classify_merge is a test-only public entry point with its own copy of the base and store guards", "recommendation": "Only tests call `classify_merge`. Its docstring describes 'a caller that knows the commit rather than the scope', which does not exist. It re-implements `classify_pending`'s base-resolution and `_unreadable_store` preamble, so most classification tests exercise a path production does not take, although `classify_commit` is shared. Separately, `classify_commit` returns `Entry` with `scope=''` and `classify` then mutates it. Passing the scope in would remove the half-built value. Both are small. Neither needs a fix unless the module is touched again.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The new release-guard step sits inside the 'One residual gap' paragraph", "recommendation": "Framework check (Instruction Clarity / Cumulative Health). The 'run `prawduct-hook check-boundary-coverage`' step is added mid-paragraph in a paragraph that opens with the residual-gap condition, so a reader could scope it to that edge case and miss the ordinary guard-fired path. The paragraph already mixed the two before this change, and most readers will take it as release-context-wide. If the paragraph is touched again, split the residual-gap note from the release-context instructions so the run-then-STOP sequence stands on its own.", "files": ["plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "pr-covered names no vouching review, while batch-covered does", "recommendation": "_batch_review returns the review id and the reason carries it. _boundary_split returns only a bool, so a pr-covered entry, which is the reading this repo's merges will almost all take, names no review B, and the --json entries carry no review id. release_readiness's own convention ('naming which evidence vouched is what lets a reader disagree with it') is the reason to return B's id here as well. That matters most once wave 2 turns the reading into a gate. Nothing reads it today, so no edit is owed now.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "classify_merge has no production caller", "recommendation": "Its docstring describes 'a caller that knows the commit rather than the scope', but only tests/test_boundary_coverage.py calls it, which makes it a test seam. It also repeats classify_pending's base and store-read preamble. The repetition costs nothing today. If the two preambles drift, a test could pass through a path the op never takes.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: covered set, no bar cleared", "recommendation": "Rated all five oracle files against the three bars: .prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md (exit 0/2/3 and the --json keys match cmd_check_boundary_coverage, _reject_unknown_args and Report.as_dict), .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-boundary-coverage-check.md, .prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-check.md, .prawduct/project-state.yaml (a comment-only change) and .prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md. None clears a bar. One phrase is imprecise: the change record says check-releasability 'prints the op's count line on every run with pending work', but an unevaluable run prints a 'could not evaluate' NOTE instead of the count line. No consumer acts on that difference, so no edit is wanted. The plan's unruled inferences are the finding above, and their subject is the code.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/boundary-coverage-check.md"], "oid": "O-10"}], "duration_seconds": 480, "s